## Chapter 6: Control Flow

Control flow in Zig feels immediately familiar to anyone who has written C, yet it removes entire categories of errors and adds expressive power that C never had. Zig’s `if`, `else`, `switch`, `for`, and `while` are not merely syntactic sugar over assembly branches; they are integrated with comptime evaluation, exhaustiveness checking, and the ability to yield values. Labels and blocks, combined with the `break` and `continue` statements, provide structured control flow that can replace many uses of `goto` and even simplify error handling patterns.

This chapter dissects each construct, examines the mechanics underneath, and explains the design decisions that make Zig’s control flow safer and more predictable than the alternatives.

---

### 6.1 Basic Usage

#### `if` and `else`

The `if` statement evaluates a boolean expression and conditionally executes a block. It can optionally include an `else` branch.

```zig
const condition: bool = true;
if (condition) {
    std.debug.print("true\n", .{});
} else {
    std.debug.print("false\n", .{});
}
```

Zig extends `if` in two important ways: it can be used as an expression, and the condition can be any value that coerces to a boolean—though Zig only allows actual `bool` values, avoiding the C footgun of integer-to-boolean coercion.

```zig
const value = if (condition) 1 else 2;
```

The branches must produce compatible types (or `noreturn`). When used as an expression, both branches are required unless the result type is `void` and one branch is omitted, which is allowed for side effects only.

#### `if` with error unions and optionals

Although error handling is covered in depth later, `if` can destructure error unions and optionals using capture syntax:

```zig
const result: anyerror!u32 = 42;
if (result) |value| {
    // value is u32
} else |err| {
    // err is the error
}
```

Similarly, for optionals (`?T`):

```zig
const maybe: ?u32 = 5;
if (maybe) |val| {
    // val is u32
} else {
    // no value present
}
```

This pattern replaces the equivalent of `if let` or `match` in other languages, and it is central to error and null handling without hidden control flow.

#### `switch`

The `switch` statement selects among multiple branches based on an integer or tagged union value. It is exhaustive by default: every possible value of the type must be handled explicitly or via an `else` catch-all.

```zig
const letter: u8 = 'A';
switch (letter) {
    'A'...'Z' => std.debug.print("uppercase\n", .{}),
    'a'...'z' => std.debug.print("lowercase\n", .{}),
    else => std.debug.print("not a letter\n", .{}),
}
```

`switch` can also be used as an expression:

```zig
const is_upper = switch (letter) {
    'A'...'Z' => true,
    else => false,
};
```

When switching over tagged unions, branches can capture the payload:

```zig
const Value = union(enum) {
    integer: i32,
    float: f64,
    none,
};

const val: Value = .{ .integer = 10 };
const description = switch (val) {
    .integer => |i| blk: {
        // i: i32
        break :blk "got integer";
    },
    .float => |f| "got float",
    .none => "nothing",
};
```

Each branch must evaluate to the same type (or `noreturn`). The compiler enforces that all variants of a tagged union are handled, eliminating entire classes of runtime bugs.

#### `while`

`while` loops repeat a block as long as a condition holds. Zig provides three forms: the classic `while (condition) { ... }`, the `while` with a continue expression, and the `while` with an optional/error union capture.

Classic:

```zig
var i: u32 = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    i += 1;
}
```

With a continue expression:

```zig
var i: u32 = 0;
while (i < 5) : (i += 1) {
    // body uses i
}
```

The continue expression is evaluated at the end of each iteration, exactly like the update clause of a C `for` loop but syntactically attached to `while` to emphasize the loop invariant.

Capturing an optional or error union:

```zig
var it = someIterator();
while (it.next()) |value| {
    // value is the payload
} else {
    // executed if the loop exits because the optional became null (no break)
}
```

This pattern elegantly combines iteration and termination detection, which in other languages would require a separate check after the loop.

#### `for`

`for` iterates over arrays, slices, and other indexable sequences. It can also iterate over ranges.

```zig
const items = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
for (items) |item| {
    std.debug.print("{d}\n", .{item});
}
```

`for` supports index capture:

```zig
for (items, 0..) |item, index| {
    std.debug.print("item {d} at {d}\n", .{ item, index });
}
```

Iterating over multiple slices simultaneously (zip-like):

```zig
const a = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 3 };
const b = [_]f32{ 1.0, 2.0, 3.0 };
for (a, b) |x, y| {
    // x: u32, y: f32
}
```

Range iteration:

```zig
for (0..5) |i| {
    // i from 0 to 4
}
```

#### Labels and Blocks

Zig allows labeling any block expression, including loops, and using `break` or `continue` with a label to jump to that block. A block that is not a loop can be broken from, providing a value.

```zig
const result = outer: {
    const x = 5;
    if (x > 3) break :outer x * 2;
    break :outer 0;
};
// result = 10
```

This replaces the need for a dedicated `goto` when you want to exit a nested scope early and return a value. It also enables patterns like labeled loops:

```zig
outer: for (0..3) |i| {
    inner: for (0..3) |j| {
        if (i * j > 2) break :outer;
    }
}
```

A `continue` can target a labeled loop:

```zig
var count: u32 = 0;
outer: while (count < 10) : (count += 1) {
    if (count == 5) continue :outer;
    // ...
}
```

This gives the programmer fine-grained control over multi-level loop control without cryptic flags or `goto`-like hacks.

