### 6.7 Best Practices

#### Use `if` with capture for error/optional unwrapping

Prefer `if (maybe) |val| { ... }` over `if (maybe != null) { const val = maybe.?; ... }`. The capture syntax is cleaner and avoids the unchecked unwrap (`.?`) on the else path.

#### Favor exhaustive `switch` over `else` chains

When handling an enum or tagged union, use a `switch` without `else` to get compile-time verification that all cases are handled. This makes your code resilient to future changes.

#### Leverage comptime `if` for platform or configuration specifics

Instead of runtime checks for compile-time-known conditions, use `if` on comptime values to prune code. It is idiomatic and ensures zero overhead.

#### Use labeled blocks to simplify nested logic

When you need to break out of a nested loop or condition and produce a value, wrap the logic in a labeled block and break with the result. This yields linear, easy-to-read code.

```zig
const found = search: {
    for (outer) |row| {
        for (row) |item| {
            if (item == target) break :search item;
        }
    }
    break :search null;
};
```

#### Prefer `for` over `while` for iterating collections

`for` conveys intent more clearly and eliminates off-by-one errors. Use `while` when the iteration condition is not simply consuming a sequence.

#### Name loop labels meaningfully

Instead of `outer` and `inner`, use labels that describe the loop’s purpose, like `row_loop` and `col_loop`. This aids comprehension.

#### Avoid `else` in `switch` unless truly necessary

A catch-all `else` branch signals that you don’t care about unknown cases. Overusing `else` defeats the exhaustiveness guarantee and can hide missing updates when the enum changes. Use with care.

#### Use `while` with continue expression for idiomatic loops

When a loop has a distinct update step, place it in the continue expression. This groups the loop-control logic and leaves the body focused on the action.

```zig
var i: usize = 0;
while (i < items.len) : (i += 1) {
    // process items[i]
}
```

